Format: `{id}{SEP}{title}{SEP}{pinned_flag}{SEP}{body}`, where SEP = "\n---\n". File
 name is `{id}.note`.

Implementation of rename: load the old file, find the note (parse it), set the new
 id and title, save it under the new name, and delete the old file. Edge case: if
 old_id == new_id, just save. If the old file doesn't exist, return NotFound.

Implementation:

```rust
pub fn rename(dir: &Path, old_id: &str, new_id: &str, new_title: &str) -> std::io
::Result<()> {
    let old_path = dir.join(format!("{}.note", old_id));
    let content = fs::read_to_string(&old_path)?;
    let parts: Vec<&str> = content.splitn(4, SEP).collect();
    if parts.len() != 4 || parts[0] != old_id {
        return Err(std::io::Error::new(
            std::io::ErrorKind::NotFound,
            format!("note not found: {old_id}"),
        ));
    }
    let note = Note {
        id: new_id.to_string(),
        title: new_title.to_string(),
        pinned: parts[2] == "1",
        body: parts[3].to_string(),
        tags: crate::extract_tags(parts[3]),
    };
    save(dir, &note)?;
    if old_id != new_id {
        fs::remove_file(&old_path)?;
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

Note: body may contain SEP, but since it's splitn(4), the body is preserved as-is
. Tags are recomputed by save? save doesn't write tags — tags are derived from body
 at load time. So recomputing tags is fine.

Also, `load_all` reads all files in the directory. No problem there.

